# Conventional Commits

Every commit subject must follow [Conventional Commits](https://www.conventionalcommits.org/):

```
<type>(optional-scope): <description>
```

Allowed types: `feat`, `fix`, `docs`, `style`, `refactor`, `perf`, `test`, `build`, `ci`, `chore`, `revert`.

Rules:

- Subject in imperative mood, lowercase description, no trailing period
- Keep the subject ≤ 72 characters
- Group a change and everything that follows from it (code, tests, docs) into one commit
- Body explains **why**, not what — omit lines that are obvious from the diff
- Breaking changes: `BREAKING CHANGE:` in the body, or `!` after the type/scope

```text
# ❌ BAD
Add chart stuff
Updated readme

# ✅ GOOD
feat(charts): add shared chart skeleton for the first workload

docs: clarify that per-cluster values live with workload charts

Argo single-source Applications require valueFiles in the same
repository as the chart.
```

When creating commits for the user, always use this format (including the HEREDOC flow from the commit workflow).
